-- title:
--   Counting C-points of a one-dimensional local algebra in a valuation ring
-- statement:
--   Let $C$ be a discrete valuation ring (a commutative domain), $K$ a field that is a fraction field of $C$, and $L$ an algebraically closed field which is an algebra over both $C$ and $K$, compatibly with the map $C \to K$, and which is algebraic over $K$. Let $A$ be a valuation subring of $L$ such that the image of every $c \in C$ in $L$ lies in $A$ (hypothesis `hCA`), and such that, for every $c \in C$, the resulting element of $A$ lies in the maximal ideal of $A$ if and only if $c$ lies in the maximal ideal of $C$; thus $A$ contains $C$ and is centred on $\mathfrak m_C$. Let $D$ be a Noetherian local commutative domain which is a $C$-algebra such that: the image in $D$ of $c \in C$ lies in $\mathfrak m_D$ exactly when $c \in \mathfrak m_C$; every $d \in D$ differs from the image of some $c \in C$ by an element of $\mathfrak m_D$ (so $D = C + \mathfrak m_D$); every nonzero prime ideal of $D$ is maximal (`Ring.DimensionLEOne D`); and $\mathfrak m_D \neq 0$. Let $\kappa$ be a field that is a fraction field of $D$, equipped with compatible $C$- and $K$-algebra structures making $C \to D \to \kappa$ and $C \to K \to \kappa$ commute, with $\kappa$ finite-dimensional and separable over $K$. Then the set of ring homomorphisms $\varphi : D \to A$ whose composite with $C \to D$ is the given map of $C$ into $A$ is finite, and its cardinality, viewed in $\mathbb N \cup \{\infty\}$, equals the $D$-module length of $D/\mathfrak m_C D$, where $\mathfrak m_C D$ denotes the ideal of $D$ generated by the image of $\mathfrak m_C$.
--
--   This is the local counting statement underlying the assertion that the geometric points of a closed point of the generic fibre which specialise to a given point of the special fibre are counted by the intersection multiplicity of its closure with the special fibre; the set being counted is $\operatorname{Hom}_C(D, A)$ and the multiplicity is $\operatorname{length}_D(D/\mathfrak m_C D)$. It is obtained by combining the count of $K$-embeddings $\kappa \to L$ with prescribed valuation ring, $\sum_B e_B f_B$, with the expression of the length of $D/\mathfrak m_C D$ as the same sum, and it is used in the construction of annuli around a node of an arithmetic curve.
